WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:05.680
Rick: "OH! I know what this is. You've been able to sustain world peace because you have one night a year

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.700
where you all run around robbing and murdering
each other without consequence."

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:09.320
Alien Hick: "That's right!"

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:09.860
Morty: "What!"

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:10.840
Rick: "It's like The Purge, Morty!"

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.220
Hey Wisecrack! Jared here. And today we're talking about future America's favorite pastime

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:16.320
Purging

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.480
At first glance, the Purge seems like
your typical horror film.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:20.660
A decent concept

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.440
"Any and all crime will be legal for 12 continuous hours."

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.480
Questionable acting.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:26.980
*questionably acts*

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.300
and all the violent carnage you can ask for.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.820
But perhaps the scariest thing about the Purge isn't the creepy masks

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:35.320
and rapist with bad teeth

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.420
but the way it highlights the dehumanizing policies
of our current social landscape.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.880
With a closer look The Purge can be considered a commentary on our sociopolitical existence.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.380
A thinely veiled allegory for America's corrupt politics rigged economic systems

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:49.700
and culture of violence.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.340
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:52.880
The Philosophy of The Purge

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.400
Summer: "Like the movie, 'The Purge'?"

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.740
Rick: "Yes, I..I need you to take th~"

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:56.860
Summer: "That movie sucked.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:58.160
Rick: "Oh my God! Hold on."

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:04.500
The Purge isn't entirely unlike other ritualistic festivals like Mardi Gras, Carnival, or even Halloween.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.940
Sure The Purge is more murder and theft and less dancing and feasting before Lent

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.020
but they're essentially the same concept.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:14.000
Designated times where people
can violate social norms.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.720
"The Purge is Halloween for adults!"

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.880
It may seem like these events are a way to

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.660
undermine the power law and religion have over people.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.460
But it's actually the opposite.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.740
They reaffirm these social structures.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:30.620
The "NFFA" or the leaders that sanction the ritual aren't actually challenged by The Purge.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:35.660
On the contrary the chaos of the night leaves people longing for the return of law the next day.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:37.620
It's kinda like a "Purge hangover".

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.820
If a ritual like carnival exists to make people accept the tenants of Christianity,

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:45.760
The Purge exists to make the people accept the tenants of the ruling social ideology.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.720
This societal order can best be described in one word:

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.020
Neoliberalism

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.500
Ruling leader: "To another successful year of the festival."

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:56.560
"Pitting poor people against each other
for thousands of years!"

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:57.780
*arrogant applause*

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.020
The politics of disposability

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.020
The basic idea of neoliberalism is that

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.840
the government should hop off the market nuts and only craft policies that promote

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.680
the free flow of goods and protect corporate interest.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.940
Each movie reflects a different facet
of the neoliberal economy.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.920
The way characters deal with The Purge

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.080
brings to focus the conditions of their
respective economic class.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.720
The original Purge is about the impact of the event on an upper middle class suburban family.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.800
The Sandin's have " 'burb " problems:

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.960
A sketchy looking homeless guy infiltrates their gated community,

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.540
their neighbors can't keep up with the Joneses so they try to kill them out jealousy,

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.240
Grace: "You made so much money off of us

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.060
and then you just stuck it in our faces."

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:37.780
their daughter is hooking up with an older dude that tries to kill daddy Sandin,

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.100
and their son is creepy as hell.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:44.240
The Purge: Anarchy focuses on the economic problems of a working class family:

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.840
Eva Sanchez can barely make ends meet,

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:50.240
can't afford medicine for her father, and desperately needs a raise.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:51.440
Tanya: "You're really gonna ask her tonight?"

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.880
Eva: "I have to...I can't afford his medicine much longer."

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.740
The inner city is full of Purge traps, gangs roaming around in taco trucks, dirt bikers kidnapping people,

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:04.540
every apartment shown involves either: attempted rape or domestic violence,

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.320
government officials round people up in 18 wheelers,

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.940
and people are abducted for some sort of remake

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:10.840
of the Running Man.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.760
Y'know. Detroit on a Tuesday.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.820
If you compare the settings of the first two films

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.280
they can be understood as a metaphor for white flight.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:22.260
The retreat of upper class white folks from the cities to the sheltered poverty-free suburbs.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.480
This abandonment exposes those left in the inner city to disproportionate amounts of:

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.580
crime, homelessness, and poverty

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:29.840
or in the case of the films

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:30.620
Purging.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:31.640
Liz: "We're downtown!"

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:33.900
"Everyone comes downtown to purge, Shane!"
Shane: "heeey, heey~"

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:37.420
For the Sandin's, the idea that they even have to deal with violence against the poor

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:39.240
is unacceptable, because...

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.980
James: "Things like this are not suppose to happen in our neighborhood."

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:43.500
The Purge: Election Year is about

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.320
the consequences of holiday on small business owners.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.620
Joe finds out that the premiums for his Purge insurance
are going up

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:50.700
for no reason.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.280
Joe: "Why you raisin' my rates now?"

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:54.060
"do y' Today~ Do you know what today is!?"

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.571
Something common when there are no government checks to stop insurance companies from

00:03:57.571 --> 00:03:58.480
"doin' you like that."

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.560
Joe: "They just raised the premium on my
Purge coverage."

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:01.500
"By thousands!"

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:02.840
"The day before the damn Purge."

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.960
"If I don't pay it by tomorrow, I have no Purge coverage for this store."

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:06.620
Marcos: "They can't do that."

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:07.600
Joe: "Yeah, well they just did it."

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.540
Taken as a whole, these examples not only illustrate the injustices of The Purge

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:13.460
but of our economic system in general.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:16.720
James: " 'kay kids. Now I know bad things do happen tonight."

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.320
"But we can afford protection."

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.080
"So we'll be fine, just like always."

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:22.080
People who prosper under neoliberalism

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:23.420
benefit from The Purge.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:25.340
And those who get fucked by neoliberalism

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.140
also get fucked by The Purge.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.800
The upper middle class largely profits from murder night

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:35.680
The Sandin's gaudy home remodel is paid for by security home systems sold to people based on the Fear

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:36.960
manifested by The Purge.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.120
Grace: "You know your husband sold a new security system"

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:42.260
"to almost every home in this community."

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:43.440
But the working class...

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:44.640
not so much.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.440
Since Eva Sanchez's boss won't give her a raise they can only protect themselves with a

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:50.000
couple flimsy 2x4s.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.200
Now that Joe can't afford Purge insurance

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.300
Joe: "I just have to protect this place myself."

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.800
He has to spend Purge night guarding his deli with little more than a shoddy rolling steel door.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.940
And eventually loses his life because of it.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:01.820
Put another way

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:06.260
The Purge exposes how neolibral economics breeds a politics of disposability.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:09.400
Something explained by thinkers Henry Giroux and Brad Evans.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:10.700
In the original Purge

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:15.000
suburbanites view entire groups of people as superfluous, disposable.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.820
It's the reason the bloody stranger is referred to as "swine" and "filth" by his foil, the "polite stranger"

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.680
"Mister and Misses, the man you're shelter is nothing but
a dirty, homeless, pig."

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:28.380
It's how James Sandin justifies incapacitating and torturing his house guest.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.740
He is merely a means to saving his family.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:36.340
It isn't until the bloody stranger volunteers to give himself up that James Finally sees him as fully human

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:38.720
and decides not to turn him over to the freaks.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.860
In The Purge: Anarchy, the unchecked price of medication

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:44.540
forces Papa Sanchez to "sell" his life to the rich

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:47.040
so his family can be eased of economic hardship.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:51.380
Papa Sanchez: "one hundred thousand dollars will be transferred into your accounts, my darlings."

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:56.300
As a disposable object his life is more valuable to his family than as an actual person.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:03.180
Eva: "It's how the wealthy Purge, baby. They buy poor and sick people and they take them in their homes

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:04.940
and they kill them where they're safe."

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:06.800
Or perhaps Rick and Morty say it best:

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:10.320
Rick: "This girl is one of your poor people and I guess you guys felt like it was okay to subject her

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:13.440
to inhuman conditions because there was no chance of it ever hurting you.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:17.800
It's sorta the sociopolitical equivalent of say a 'suit of power armor' around you.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:20.560
But now things are even out sooo... Arthricia?"

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:26.800
Giroux and Evans argue that people become disposable under an economic system that is characterized by

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:32.800
privatization of things like: Healthcare, the deregulation of banks, and the erosion of government safety nets like

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:34.440
social security and welfare.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:39.880
Dante: "For the past twenty years the NFFA has taken to legalize murder to decrease the poor population

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:44.720
which in turn keeps the government's spending down. Less welfare, less healthcare, less housing."

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:48.880
If The Purge stands as a one night reference point for the terrors of such policies

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:52.080
then shutting down medical and police services makes sense.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:55.200
'Cause government policy can't possibly be more
lax than that.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:58.580
All of this is justified under the banner of
economic growth.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:02.020
Cali: "They're keeping the population down by getting rid of people like us, to save money."

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:05.980
The elderly infirmed poor and so-called "non-contributors"

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:08.960
are sacrificed so that the economy can grow unhindered.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.440
Dante: "These are the real victims of The Purge. People that the NFFA are trying to eliminate

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.380
so that they don't have to take care of them anymore."

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:19.740
This elimination of the population is actually paralleled in the real neoliberal economy:

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:24.780
people unable to afford Epipens, people stuck eating ramen and the dollar menu for 20 years,

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:30.280
or people exposed to contaminated drinking water, pollution from coal factory plants, and nuclear waste.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:33.860
Neoliberalism exposes those same populations
to slow death.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:37.160
Instead of actively Purging them it just sorta lets them die.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:41.660
This justification for The Purge isn't based some new fancy economic idea.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:46.980
It goes all the way back to 1798 in a book titled "Essay on the Principle of Population"

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:50.540
by Reverend Thomas Robert Malthus
(a.k.a Reverend Run).

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:54.340
Malthus argued that because production would be unable to keep up with demand

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:57.640
a growing population would create
massive food shotages.

00:07:57.700 --> 00:07:58.560
His solution:

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:00.820
Employ laws that keep birth rates low

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:02.260
and death rates high.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:04.720
Things like famine, disease, and wars.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:07.660
Big Daddy: "Unfortunately the citizens aren't killing enough.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:10.540
So we supplement it all...to keep things balanced."

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:12.340
This is echoed in The Purge: Election Year

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:14.820
by Caleb Warrens, head of the NFFA:

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:23.900
"Idealistic pigs! They want the impossible. Everyone to HAVE. SOME cannot have.

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:26.180
NOT enough to go around."

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:27.740
But does this logic hold up?

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:30.100
For Friedrich Engels of the hip-hop duo

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:31.340
"commie much beard"

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:33.060
Malthus had it all wrong.

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:38.200
The issue isn't increasing population it's an economic system that doesn't value workers.

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:40.840
Malthus was putting economics above people.

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:43.260
Essentially Engels argued that Malthus' model

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:46.000
was nothing more than an excuse
to be a dick to poor people.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.200
With their over the top Purge ceremonies

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:52.040
the NFFA clearly have more than economic concerns on the mind.

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:54.840
They take "being a dick to poor people" to the next level.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:56.060
"AAAAAHHH!!"

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:56.840
"BORN!"

00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:58.040
"AGAIN!!"

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:02.980
Neoliberal policies are confusing in the abstract. There's a bunch of economic theory and political

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:04.560
jargon you're suppose to know.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:09.000
But the genius of The Purge is the way that it uses "murder night" to viscerally expose

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:12.600
how this kind of economy generates profit in a predatory manner.

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:18.040
Carmelo: "Profit making is not the essence of democracy. Wake up people. Wake up!"

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:23.071
The privilege make money hand over fist on: weapon sales, insurance, private body guards,

00:09:23.071 --> 00:09:24.500
and home security systems

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:27.300
while the poor have to fight to simply survive.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:28.780
Conclusion

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:33.380
The Purge movies may present an opportunity to peek into a not-so-distant dystopia.

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:36.780
Except most of the main characters don't enjoy Purging much.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:38.660
The Rick and Morty episode about Purging

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:40.560
provides an interesting counterpoint.

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:45.240
Rick's sick enjoyment of the brutal violence says more about audience than the films.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:48.280
Rick: "If there's a video online with someone getting decapitated, you don't click on it?

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:50.180
Morty: "No! Wh~Why would I do that?

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:51.120
"You do that?"

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.340
Rick: "I don't. Because it would bore me. I see sh*t like that for breakfast, Morty."

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:57.740
Sitting in front of any of The Purge movies puts us in Rick's position.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:01.480
"Oh my god! This is f*cking awesome! Morty! This is really cool!"

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:03.900
Perhaps the reality is we're the murder tourist.

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:05.440
Perusing through each sequel

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:09.180
excitingly waiting for a titillating peek at something
shocking and new.

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:12.880
After all: Does anybody actually remember any of the characters of these movies?

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:14.460
Liz: "We don't even know your names."

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:18.200
Do we all go see the next Purge movie because we can't wait to see what happens next

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:19.580
with the new founding fathers?

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:21.780
Or perhaps the success of The Purge films

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:23.860
reveals what most of us already know:

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:28.620
People like watching violent shit because... well sometimes it just feels good.

